== Strategy & Tactics: Intermediate

This guide assumes you completed a campaign on Normal. Here we cover army composition, battle tactics, agents, trade, and how to survive until Realm Divide.

=== Phase 2: Military Mastery

==== Army Composition by Era

Your army changes as the game progresses. Do not use the same army at turn 10 and turn 60.

*Early game (turns 1-20, castle tiers 1-2):*
8 yari ashigaru (anvil), 4 bow ashigaru (support), 2 yari cavalry (flank), 1 general. This costs roughly 1,000-1,200 koku per turn. Do not deviate from this. You cannot afford samurai.

*Mid game (turns 20-50, castle tier 3):*
4 yari ashigaru (keep them for yari wall), 4 katana samurai (the new anvil once lines are pinned), 2-3 naginata samurai (anti-cavalry reserve), 4 bow samurai (replaces bow ashigaru), 4 cavalry (2 yari + 2 katana cavalry), 1 general. Costs roughly 2,500-3,500 koku per turn. Replace units as they die, not all at once.

*Late game (turns 50+, castle tier 4):*
2 yari ashigaru (still useful for yari wall), 6 katana samurai, 4 naginata samurai, 2 matchlock ashigaru or samurai, 4 bow samurai, 4 katana cavalry, 1 general. Costs 5,000+ koku per turn. At this point you should have 3-4 such armies.

==== The Unit Triangle: Exact Mechanics

Every unit in Shogun 2 has three stats that determine matchups:

*Melee attack:* The chance to land a hit per swing. Katana samurai: 12-14. Yari ashigaru: 4-6. A katana samurai hits twice as often as an ashigaru.

*Melee defense:* The chance to block an incoming hit. Yari ashigaru in yari wall: +4 bonus (total 8-10). Katana samurai: 10-12. Naginata: 14-16 (best defensive unit in the game).

*Charge bonus:* Added to melee attack on the first hit after a charge. Cavalry: 15-25. Katana samurai: 5-8. This is why cavalry must charge in and pull out — the bonus only applies on the first hit, then degrades.

The rock-paper-scissors:
- *Yari beat cavalry* because yari wall gives +15 bonus vs cavalry charge and negates the enemy's charge bonus entirely. A cavalry unit charging yari wall attacks at its base melee attack (10-12) against the yari's melee defense (8-10) plus the +15 wall bonus — the cavalry cannot hit reliably while the yari chews them up.
- *Katana beat yari* because katana samurai have 2x the melee attack of yari ashigaru and high armor (6-8 vs yari's 2-3). They cut through the yari's defense faster than the yari can retaliate.
- *Cavalry beat ranged and flank* because ranged units have zero melee defense and cannot fight back. A cavalry charge into the rear of any unit adds a -8 morale penalty and negates shields, making the charge 3x more effective than a frontal charge.

==== Matchlock Units: Timing and Line of Sight

Matchlock ashigaru and samurai (available at castle tier 3-4 with the gunpowder tech researched) are the most powerful ranged units in the game IF you use them correctly.

- Matchlocks have a flat trajectory. They cannot shoot over your own infantry. You must create gaps in your front line for matchlocks to shoot through, or position them on a flank.
- Matchlocks have extremely long reload time. One volley every 20-30 seconds. Make each volley count.
- Matchlocks cause morale shock. A unit hit by a matchlock volley takes a -2 to -4 morale penalty on top of casualties. Two volleys into the same unit will cause it to waver.
- Matchlocks are useless in rain. Rain reduces their accuracy by ~80%. Check the weather overlay before deploying.

To use matchlocks effectively: deploy your yari line in a shallow formation (2 ranks wide), place matchlocks 10-15 units behind the gaps between yari units. The matchlocks shoot through the gaps. When the enemy infantry reaches your line, order your matchlocks to flank and shoot into the enemy's flank — they will not hit your own troops this way.

==== Advanced Battle Tactics

*Hammer and Anvil (standard):*
1. Yari advances and engages the enemy line. Formation: yari wall, 4 ranks deep.
2. Cavalry moves wide — outside the enemy's line of sight if possible (use forests, hills).
3. Cavalry charges the rear of an engaged enemy unit. The target routes almost instantly.
4. The routing unit causes nearby enemies to check morale. Often 2-3 more units rout.
5. Repeat until the entire enemy line breaks.

*Defense in Depth (outnumbered):*
1. Deploy on a hill. High ground gives a combat bonus (roughly +2 melee attack to the uphill side).
2. Place yari at the hill's crest, bows on the reverse slope. Enemy bow units cannot shoot what they cannot see.
3. When the enemy charges uphill, they arrive exhausted (fatigue penalty). Exhausted units fight at ~50% effectiveness.
4. Counter-charge downhill with cavalry for maximum charge bonus.

*The Feigned Retreat (advanced, multiplayer-proven):*
1. Select a cheap unit (yari ashigaru) and order them to retreat through a gap in your line.
2. The enemy AI often pursues the retreating unit, breaking their formation.
3. Hit the exposed enemy flank with your cavalry or katana reserve.

==== Agent Operations: Skill Trees and Strategy

-- Metsuke --

Metsuke are your economic backbone. Every metsuke should specialize:

*Economic metsuke (primary):*
- Skill: Strengthen Town (every turn in your wealthiest province)
- Skill: Oversight (passive tax bonus in the province)
- Skill: Crackdown (suppress enemy agents)
- Promotion path: stay in one province until rank 4, then rotate to the next wealthiest
- A rank-4 economic metsuke adds roughly 30-50% to a province's tax output

*Enforcement metsuke (secondary):*
- Skill: Arrest (remove enemy ninja and monks)
- Skill: Investigate (find enemy agents)
- Counter-agent role. Place in provinces with high enemy agent activity.
- Can also be embedded in armies to root out enemy agents on the campaign map

*Oversight metsuke (tertiary — late game):*
- Skill: Oversight (all levels)
- Station in military recruitment provinces. Reduces recruitment cost and time.
- A rank-3 oversight metsuke can reduce recruitment costs by ~20%.

-- Ninja --

Ninja are your offensive arm. Each ninja should have ONE primary role:

*Assassination ninja:*
- Skills: Way of the Wind (movement), Shadow (assassination chance), Silent Strike (bonus vs generals)
- Target: enemy generals (removes the army's morale anchor), enemy metsuke (opening their provinces), enemy monks
- Never use your assassination ninja for sabotage — they level slower on sabotage XP

*Sabotage ninja:*
- Skills: Sabotage (buildings), Arson (castle damage), Way of the Earth (survival)
- Target: enemy recruitment buildings (prevents new units), castle walls (reduces siege defense)
- A high-rank sabotage ninja can destroy a castle's walls in one turn, making siege assault trivial

*Scout ninja:*
- Skills: Way of the Wind (movement), Way of Water (rivers), Eyes of the Lord (visibility)
- Use: keep ahead of your main army, scout enemy army composition, detect ambushes
- Place in forests near expected enemy approach routes

-- Monks --

Monks handle religion and morale. Every province has a religion split between Shinto and Buddhism (plus Christianity if you convert).

*Religious conversion (primary):*
- Your starting clan has a state religion (Shimazu: Shinto, Oda: Shinto, Tokugawa: Shinto).
- Provinces with your state religion get +3-5 happiness.
- Provinces with opposing religions stack penalties: -3 per non-state religion present.
- A province with Shinto 80% and Buddhism 20% has Buddhism at 20%: -3 happiness.
- Use your monk's Convert action each turn in provinces with the wrong religion.

*Warrior monk (combat embed):*
- Skills: Warrior (melee attack), Inspiration (morale boost to nearby units), Levies (recruit monk units)
- Embed in your main army. The Inspiration aura adds +4 morale to nearby units.
- Monks can also recruit Warrior Monk units — some of the best infantry in the game (high attack, high morale, causes fear).
- A rank-3 Warrior Monk embedded in an army turns a standard army into an elite force.

*Inciter (offensive):*
- Skills: Incite Revolt (reduces happiness in target province), Insurrection (triggers a rebel army)
- Use: target a province you plan to attack. Incite revolt reduces the garrison's morale. If a rebel army spawns, it fights the garrison — your army arrives afterward to mop up.

==== Trade and Diplomacy

-- Trade Nodes --

There are four offshore trade nodes on the naval map. Each provides trade goods worth 500-1,500 koku per turn depending on the node. Sending a trade ship to a node establishes a trade route. The ship auto-returns income each turn.

- Control of trade nodes is the difference between a struggling economy and a booming one.
- Each trade node can support 4 trade ships from your faction.
- Enemy clan trade nodes can be attacked. Sinking a trade ship does not declare war; blockading a node does.
- The Black Ship (Portuguese, appears near Kyushu in late game) is not a trade node but a unique ship. Capture it by boarding — it dominates any naval engagement for the rest of the game.

-- Diplomacy Mechanics --

Diplomacy operates on a hidden relationship score (-200 to +200). Every action modifies this score:

*Positive actions:* honoring alliances (+30), giving gifts (+5 per 1,000 koku), marrying a daughter (+20), granting military access (+15), liberating captured provinces (+40 to the liberated clan)

*Negative actions:* breaking alliances (-40), refuse alliance request (-10), attacking allies (-80), trespassing (-5 per turn), territory expansion near their borders (-2 per province per turn)

*Marriage:* Marrying a daughter to another clan's daimyo creates an alliance that lasts 15-25 turns. After that, the alliance may break. Marriage does not prevent war — it delays it. Use marriage to secure one border while expanding on the other.

*Alliance strategy:* Never have more than 2 active alliances. Each alliance reduces the Realm Divide trigger threshold by roughly 2 provinces. Having 4 alliances means Realm Divide triggers at ~18 provinces instead of ~25.

==== Wave 1 Progression (Oda Clan)

1. *Turns 1-15:* Unify Owari province. Build farms and markets. Recruit ashigaru core. Do NOT attack the Ikko-Ikki early — they have high happiness and multiple stacks.
2. *Turns 15-30:* Expand east toward Kyoto. Take one province per 4 turns. Use marriage to keep the western clans (Mori, Otomo) neutral.
3. *Turns 30-50:* Capture Kyoto. The shogunate will be angry. Do not let yourself become Shogun yet — wait until you have 4 full armies.
4. *Turns 50-80:* Expand west and north simultaneously. Fortify Kyoto. Build trading ports and claim the western trade nodes.
5. *Turns 80+:* Realm Divide should trigger at ~25 provinces. You want 4 full armies, 20,000+ koku, and strong garrisons on all borders before this happens.
